      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's dive into the mechanics of the vocal cords without the goblin metaphors. When you produce the s sound, as in \"sun,\" your vocal cords do not vibrate. This is known as a voiceless sound. The air flows over the closed vocal cords, creating a turbulent airflow that results in the sibilant sound. In contrast, when you produce the z sound, as in \"zebra,\" your vocal cords come together and vibrate. This vibration is what creates the voiced sound. The difference in vibration between the two sounds is due to the tension and position of the vocal cords. For the s sound, the vocal cords are relaxed and do not vibrate, while for the z sound, the vocal cords are brought together and vibrate as air passes through them. This vibration adds a buzzing quality to the z sound, distinguishing it from the s sound.",
